<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Bahamas</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Commonwealth of The Bahamas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bahamas is an archipelagic country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, within the Lucayan Archipelago in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. The islands were inhabited by the Arawak and Lucayans, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> shipped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> and enslaved, leaving the islands mostly deserted from 1513 until 1648. English colonists settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleuthera">Eleuthera</a> in 1649, and the Bahamas became a crown colony in 1718. After the American Revolutionary War, Loyalists brought slaves with them; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was abolished in 1834. The Bahamas became an independent Commonwealth realm in 1973, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling">Lynden Pindling</a> as its first prime minister.</p></div>
